WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.310
My name is Navid Abedzadeh.

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.500
And I've been working
on the development

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.870
of a lossless electron
beam splitter, which

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:10.090
could be ultimately used in
quantum electron microscopy.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.210
So traditionally the way
electron beam splitting

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:16.020
has worked is by passing
a beam of electrons

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.360
through a thin crystal.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.650
The periodic atomic
structure of the crystal

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.650
would diffract the electron
beam and therefore split it.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.830
The problem with
that is that whenever

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.750
there is electron
going through matter,

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.260
there is the inevitable losses
due to inelastic scattering

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.700
and absorption.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.100
Our approach is to use
a diffractive electron

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.290
mirror, which is a
nanofabricated diffraction

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.300
grating with a negative
bias applied to it.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.050
So what happens is sinusoidal
potential surfaces form

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.574
on top of this
diffraction grating.

00:00:44.574 --> 00:00:46.990
And when an electron interacts
with it, two things happen.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:49.650
First of all, because these
surfaces are negatively biased,

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.060
the electron gets reflected.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.280
And secondly, because they
are sinusoidal in shape,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.510
the electron gets diffracted.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.886
And that's where the
splitting happens.

00:00:57.886 --> 00:00:59.760
And since we didn't
really go through matter,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.384
all the losses that
I mentioned earlier

00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:03.820
are not a part of this design.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.900
So ultimately the hope is to use
this device in quantum electron

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.260
microscopy, which is a
novel electron microscopy

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.670
technique proposed to allow
for nanometer resolution

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.390
imaging of sensitive biological
samples, such as proteins,

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:23.390
without all the damage that's
caused by the electron beam.

